     - test C (true race with latch): both concurrent; assert exactly one of: (confirm 200 && decisions==1 && latest closure OPEN created after lift) or (confirm 409 && decisions==0 && review INVALIDATED). And always: decisions ≤ 1, closures consistent.

   For concurrent confirmation test, need two threads calling service.confirm (or MockMvc). MockMvc is thread-safe for concurrent performs? MockMvc is generally thread-safe. But simpler to call service bean directly. I'll autowire SnowService in concurrency test and call confirm directly with CountDownLatch start gate, ExecutorService, futures with timeout 10s.

   Note H2 FOR UPDATE row lock: second tx waits; H2 default lock timeout is 1-2 seconds? H2 default LOCK_TIMEOUT is 2000ms? Actually H2 2.x default lock timeout is 2000 ms? Hmm, DEFAULT_LOCK_TIMEOUT... In H2 2.x, LOCK_TIMEOUT default is 2000ms? Let me think — H2 docs: LOCK_TIMEOUT default 2000 (ms)? Historically H2 default was 2000ms. Concurrent test with serialized short transactions should be well under that. OK.

   Deadlock risk: both txs lock same single row → no deadlock, just wait.

Also unique constraint test: "SQL、唯一约束...在H2上实际验证" — concurrent confirmations hitting uq_confirmation_review_reviewer? The duplicate-reviewer path is app-level. Unique constraint on lift_decision.review_id guards decision duplication. With serialization it won't trigger, but constraint exists. Fine.

Now about MockMvc + @SpringBootTest: mock web environment default. @AutoConfigureMockMvc. Good — no server started.

One issue: `ensureArea` uses `INSERT ... SELECT ... FROM DUAL WHERE NOT EXISTS` — H2 supports SELECT ... FROM DUAL. In MySQL mode DUAL is supported. OK.

Another: schema.sql `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` with `COMMENT ON` — H2 supports COMMENT ON TABLE/COLUMN. Yes.

H2 `AUTO_INCREMENT` in MySQL mode works.

KeyHolder with preparedStatement(sql, new String[]{"id"}) — H2 returns generated keys. OK.

Now `spring.sql.init` runs schema.sql at startup for both main and test. In test, datasource url overridden. sql.init.mode=always inherited. Good.

Jackson time-zone UTC — Instant serialized as timestamps? Spring Boot default writes Instant as ISO-8601 string (WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS disabled by Boot). Our DTOs use String for instants anyway.

Validation: @NotBlank on record components — need `spring-boot-starter-validation` (present) and @Valid. OK.

Error handling for malformed JSON / missing body → Spring defaults (400). Fine.

One more: ApiExceptionHandler should also handle HttpMessageNotReadableException → 400? Default handles it. Fine.

Now write test files. Base class first.

